## 4. Phase 3: Reliability & Dashboard Accuracy (P1/P2)
**Objective:** Ensure the staff experience is accurate, reflects server-state, and fails gracefully.

| Task ID | Component | Remediation Action | Est. | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1** | **Server-Side Resolution** | **Problem:** Currently, when a staff member clicks "Resolve" on the dashboard, the application only updates temporary local browser storage (a Zustand/CRDT store state called `resolveComplaintLocally`). The PostgreSQL database has no idea the complaint was resolved, meaning SLAs will still show as breaching, and reports will be wrong.<br><br>**Solution:** We need to build a true end-to-end functionality:<br>1. Create a Secure Server Action `resolveComplaint(id)`.<br>2. This action must authenticate the user, make sure only valid complaints are being closed, and execute an `UPDATE complaints SET status = 'resolved'` query on the database.<br>3. Once updated, the database triggers will automatically write a record to the tamper-proof `complaint_status_history` audit ledger.<br>4. Change the "Resolve" button in the UI to call this API (showing a loading spinner) instead of just updating local state. | 4h | Todo |
| **3.2** | **Remove Mock Data** | Replace mock fallbacks with an error state screen when the database fetch fails. | 1h | Todo |
| **3.3** | **Redis Fail-Safe** | Refactor `rate-limit.ts` so an unavailable Redis connection doesn't instantly crash the intake API. | 3h | Todo |
| **3.4** | **Admin/DPO Access** | Allow users with the `admin` or `dpo` roles to open PHI complaints regardless of department restrictions. | 1h | Todo |

